  "diagramStyle": "diagrams/data-drift-monitoring.css",
  "hero": "<section class=\"ddm-hero\"><span class=\"ddm-kicker\">DATA DRIFT MONITORING · VISUAL STUDY</span><h2>How do we detect changes in the data a model receives?</h2><p>Compare meaningful reference and live windows, test distribution changes, inspect segments, and investigate alerts before modifying models.</p><div class=\"ddm-art\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 1000 430\" role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"ddm-head-0 ddm-desc-0\"><title id=\"ddm-head-0\">Did the live data change after deployment?</title><desc id=\"ddm-desc-0\">Age-like feature values in two windows; baseline bin counts four six zero zero and current counts zero three six one reveal a shift, not necessarily model failure.</desc><rect width=\"1000\" height=\"430\" fill=\"#f8fcfd\"/><text x=\"37\" y=\"40\" font-size=\"14\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"#268475\" font-weight=\"850\" font-family=\"system-ui,Segoe UI,Arial,sans-serif\">DATA DRIFT MONITORING · VISUAL STUDY</text><text x=\"37\" y=\"79\" font-size=\"25\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"#2a5267\" font-weight=\"850\" font-family=\"system-ui,Segoe UI,Arial,sans-serif\">Did the live data change after deployment?</text><rect x=\"28\" y=\"111\" width=\"943\" height=\"267\" rx=\"12\" fill=\"#fff\" stroke=\"#d5e4ea\" stroke-width=\"1.3\"/><text x=\"54\" y=\"150\" font-size=\"16\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"#528197\" font-weight=\"850\" font-family=\"system-ui,Segoe UI,Arial,sans-serif\">ONE FEATURE · TWO DIFFERENT TIME WINDOWS</text><rect x=\"73\" y=\"219\" width=\"28\" height=\"100\" rx=\"3\" fill=\"#5dadd0\" stroke=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.3\"/><rect x=\"106\" y=\"319\" width=\"28\" height=\"0\" rx=\"3\" fill=\"#e8a270\" stroke=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.3\"/><rect x=\"189\" y=\"169\" width=\"28\" height=\"150\" rx=\"3\" fill=\"#5dadd0\" stroke=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.3\"/><rect x=\"222\" y=\"244\" width=\"28\" height=\"75\" rx=\"3\" fill=\"#e8a270\" stroke=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.3\"/><rect x=\"305\" y=\"319\" width=\"28\" height=\"0\" rx=\"3\" fill=\"#5dadd0\" stroke=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.3\"/><rect x=\"338\" y=\"169\" width=\"28\" height=\"150\" rx=\"3\" fill=\"#e8a270\" stroke=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.3\"/><rect x=\"421\" y=\"319\" width=\"28\" height=\"0\" rx=\"3\" fill=\"#5dadd0\" stroke=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.3\"/><rect x=\"454\" y=\"294\" width=\"28\" height=\"25\" rx=\"3\" fill=\"#e8a270\" stroke=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.3\"/><line x1=\"66\" y1=\"321\" x2=\"503\" y2=\"321\" stroke=\"#b1cbd5\" stroke-width=\"2\" /><circle cx=\"76\" cy=\"354\" r=\"6\" fill=\"#5dadd0\"/><text x=\"89\" y=\"359\" font-size=\"15\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"#487c93\" font-weight=\"730\" font-family=\"system-ui,Segoe UI,Arial,sans-serif\">Reference</text><circle cx=\"221\" cy=\"354\" r=\"6\" fill=\"#e8a270\"/><text x=\"233\" y=\"359\" font-size=\"15\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"#976c52\" font-weight=\"730\" font-family=\"system-ui,Segoe UI,Arial,sans-serif\">Recent</text><text x=\"104\" y=\"342\" font-size=\"14\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#547c8d\" font-weight=\"680\" font-family=\"system-ui,Segoe UI,Arial,sans-serif\">≤45</text><text x=\"220\" y=\"342\" font-size=\"14\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#547c8d\" font-weight=\"680\" font-family=\"system-ui,Segoe UI,Arial,sans-serif\">46–55</text><text x=\"336\" y=\"342\" font-size=\"14\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#547c8d\" font-weight=\"680\" font-family=\"system-ui,Segoe UI,Arial,sans-serif\">56–65</text><text x=\"452\" y=\"342\" font-size=\"14\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#547c8d\" font-weight=\"680\" font-family=\"system-ui,Segoe UI,Arial,sans-serif\">66+</text><rect x=\"551\" y=\"153\" width=\"382\" height=\"205\" rx=\"15\" fill=\"#eaf8f1\" stroke=\"#d2e7db\" stroke-width=\"1.3\"/><text x=\"574\" y=\"191\" font-size=\"16\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"#4b7d84\" font-weight=\"850\" font-family=\"system-ui,Segoe UI,Arial,sans-serif\">REFERENCE → RECENT</text><text x=\"574\" y=\"244\" font-size=\"25\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"#2d897f\" font-weight=\"860\" font-family=\"system-ui,Segoe UI,Arial,sans-serif\">Distribution changed</text><text x=\"574\" y=\"284\" font-size=\"19\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"#4d717f\" font-weight=\"700\" font-family=\"system-ui,Segoe UI,Arial,sans-serif\">Monitoring finds the shift.</text><text x=\"574\" y=\"318\" font-size=\"17\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"#657d88\" font-weight=\"650\" font-family=\"system-ui,Segoe UI,Arial,sans-serif\">It does not prove lower accuracy.</text></svg></div><p class=\"ddm-note\">All feature values and results are illustrative; a drift alert is not proof of a quality failure.</p></section>",
  "questions": [
    {
      "stem": "What is Data Drift Monitoring in MLOps, and why does comparing live feature distributions with reference data help detect problems before ground-truth labels arrive?",
      "tag": "Beginner · Definition and purpose",
      "answer": "c",
      "options": [
        "Data Drift Monitoring compares recent model accuracy with training accuracy to detect a drop before new labels arrive. Feature distributions are secondary because model performance can be observed directly without outcomes.",
        "Data Drift Monitoring checks changes in live feature averages after deployment. A stable average is enough to establish distribution stability, so quantile and category-frequency checks can be skipped.",
        "Data Drift Monitoring compares live input distributions with a representative reference. It identifies changes before outcomes arrive but cannot establish that prediction accuracy has declined.",
        "Data Drift Monitoring compares current predicted labels with earlier predicted labels and treats different class frequencies as confirmed errors. This avoids needing a reference dataset or independent checks of input features."
      ],
      "explanation": "<strong>Explanation:</strong> Data Drift Monitoring checks whether the distribution of live input features differs from a chosen reference period. It can run before true outcome labels arrive because it compares feature values rather than prediction correctness. In a grocery-delivery app, order sizes may move from small weekday baskets to large holiday baskets. The change deserves investigation but does not automatically mean the prediction model has failed. Compare feature distributions, inspect affected users, and measure real outcomes when they become available.",
      "figure": "<section class=\"ddm-figure\"><span class=\"ddm-kicker\">QUESTION 01 · VISUAL LESSON</span><div class=\"ddm-art\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 1000 430\" role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"ddm-head-1 ddm-desc-1\"><title id=\"ddm-head-1\">Data drift is a change in live input distributions</title><desc id=\"ddm-desc-1\">Reference input values and recent production values are compared to detect P(X) changes; a shift does not by itself prove concept drift or degraded accuracy.</desc><rect width=\"1000\" height=\"430\" fill=\"#f8fcfd\"/><text x=\"37\" y=\"40\" font-size=\"14\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"#268475\" font-weight=\"850\" font-family=\"system-ui,Segoe UI,Arial,sans-serif\">01 · DATA DRIFT MONITORING</text><text x=\"37\" y=\"79\" font-size=\"25\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"#2a5267\" font-weight=\"850\" font-family=\"system-ui,Segoe UI,Arial,sans-serif\">Data drift is a change in live input distributions</text><rect x=\"28\" y=\"111\" width=\"942\" height=\"268\" rx=\"12\" fill=\"#fff\" stroke=\"#d5e4ea\" stroke-width=\"1.3\"/><text x=\"52\" y=\"152\" font-size=\"16\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"#568093\" font-weight=\"850\" font-family=\"system-ui,Segoe UI,Arial,sans-serif\">A ROUTINE COMPARISON, NOT A MODEL RETRAIN</text><rect x=\"51\" y=\"179\" width=\"266\" height=\"133\" rx=\"12\" fill=\"#ecf8fc\" stroke=\"#d5e4ea\" stroke-width=\"1.3\"/><rect x=\"367\" y=\"179\" width=\"266\" height=\"133\" rx=\"12\" fill=\"#eef9f2\" stroke=\"#d5e4ea\" stroke-width=\"1.3\"/><rect x=\"683\" y=\"179\" width=\"263\" height=\"133\" rx=\"12\" fill=\"#fff3e9\" stroke=\"#d5e4ea\" stroke-width=\"1.3\"/><text x=\"184\" y=\"217\" font-size=\"18\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#367e99\" font-weight=\"850\" font-family=\"system-ui,Segoe UI,Arial,sans-serif\">REFERENCE</text><text x=\"184\" y=\"253\" font-size=\"17\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#517c8c\" font-weight=\"710\" font-family=\"system-ui,Segoe UI,Arial,sans-serif\">Past typical inputs</text><text x=\"500\" y=\"217\" font-size=\"18\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#2c8b7b\" font-weight=\"850\" font-family=\"system-ui,Segoe UI,Arial,sans-serif\">CURRENT</text><text x=\"500\" y=\"253\" font-size=\"17\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#517c8c\" font-weight=\"710\" font-family=\"system-ui,Segoe UI,Arial,sans-serif\">Live feature values</text><text x=\"814\" y=\"217\" font-size=\"18\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#b77b51\" font-weight=\"850\" font-family=\"system-ui,Segoe UI,Arial,sans-serif\">INVESTIGATE</text><text x=\"814\" y=\"253\" font-size=\"17\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#517c8c\" font-weight=\"710\" font-family=\"system-ui,Segoe UI,Arial,sans-serif\">When they differ</text><line x1=\"323\" y1=\"245\" x2=\"360\" y2=\"245\" stroke=\"#638e9f\" stroke-width=\"3\" /><path d=\"M350 237 L360 245 L350 253\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#638e9f\" stroke-width=\"3\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\"/><line x1=\"638\" y1=\"245\" x2=\"675\" y2=\"245\" stroke=\"#638e9f\" stroke-width=\"3\" /><path d=\"M665 237 L675 245 L665 253\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#638e9f\" stroke-width=\"3\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\"/><text x=\"56\" y=\"355\" font-size=\"17\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"#5d7b89\" font-weight=\"680\" font-family=\"system-ui,Segoe UI,Arial,sans-serif\">The label or model performance may be unchanged even when P(X) shifts.</text></svg></div><p class=\"ddm-note\">Distribution drift concerns P(X); predictive-quality loss must be evaluated separately.</p></section>"
    },
    {
      "stem": "How should reference and current data windows be chosen for Data Drift Monitoring, and why do seasonality, sample size and data quality affect the comparison?",
      "tag": "Beginner · Reference and current windows",
      "answer": "a",
      "options": [
        "Use comparable reference and recent windows with enough records. Check seasonality, missing values and schema consistency before treating a difference between their feature distributions as meaningful.",
        "Always use the full original training dataset as the reference, even if its population differs from deployment. Comparing live data against training guarantees any measured drift reflects a model problem.",
        "Use whichever recent time window creates the biggest difference from a historical period. Large observed changes improve alert sensitivity, and consistent schema checks are unnecessary once timestamps match.",
        "Compare live data against the immediately preceding batch regardless of sample size or seasonal context. A moving reference ensures permanent shifts cannot be missed and makes normal fluctuations irrelevant."
      ],
      "explanation": "<strong>Explanation:</strong> A reference should represent normal deployment conditions and have enough examples to describe important features. A current window needs enough observations for reliable comparison. Check feature types, missing values, duplicates and expected seasonal patterns. For a city-transit passenger forecast, comparing a regular January weekday to another weekday is more meaningful than comparing it to a festival weekend and interpreting the difference as a failure. Version reference datasets so alerts remain understandable.",
      "figure": "<section class=\"ddm-figure\"><span class=\"ddm-kicker\">QUESTION 02 · VISUAL LESSON</span><div class=\"ddm-art\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 1000 430\" role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"ddm-head-2 ddm-desc-2\"><title id=\"ddm-head-2\">The reference window must match normal operating conditions</title><desc id=\"ddm-desc-2\">Three time windows compare weekday-to-weekday against a noncomparable festival weekend; selecting proper samples and seasonality matters.</desc><rect width=\"1000\" height=\"430\" fill=\"#f8fcfd\"/><text x=\"37\" y=\"40\" font-size=\"14\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"#268475\" font-weight=\"850\" font-family=\"system-ui,Segoe UI,Arial,sans-serif\">02 · DATA DRIFT MONITORING</text><text x=\"37\" y=\"79\" font-size=\"25\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"#2a5267\" font-weight=\"850\" font-family=\"system-ui,Segoe UI,Arial,sans-serif\">The reference window must match normal operating conditions</text><rect x=\"28\" y=\"110\" width=\"941\" height=\"272\" rx=\"12\" fill=\"#fff\" stroke=\"#d5e4ea\" stroke-width=\"1.3\"/><text x=\"50\" y=\"149\" font-size=\"16\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"#548196\" font-weight=\"850\" font-family=\"system-ui,Segoe UI,Arial,sans-serif\">CHOOSE WINDOWS THAT REPRESENT THE SAME TYPE OF TRAFFIC</text><rect x=\"49\" y=\"165\" width=\"284\" height=\"133\" rx=\"12\" fill=\"#eaf7fc\" stroke=\"#d5e4ea\" stroke-width=\"1.3\"/><text x=\"191\" y=\"202\" font-size=\"17\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#3b849a\" font-weight=\"850\" font-family=\"system-ui,Segoe UI,Arial,sans-serif\">JANUARY WEEKDAY</text><text x=\"191\" y=\"242\" font-size=\"17\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#57798a\" font-weight=\"720\" font-family=\"system-ui,Segoe UI,Arial,sans-serif\">Reference: 2,000 trips</text><rect x=\"365\" y=\"165\" width=\"284\" height=\"133\" rx=\"12\" fill=\"#ebf9f1\" stroke=\"#d5e4ea\" stroke-width=\"1.3\"/><text x=\"507\" y=\"202\" font-size=\"17\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#378975\" font-weight=\"850\" font-family=\"system-ui,Segoe UI,Arial,sans-serif\">JANUARY WEEKDAY</text><text x=\"507\" y=\"242\" font-size=\"17\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#57798a\" font-weight=\"720\" font-family=\"system-ui,Segoe UI,Arial,sans-serif\">Recent: 2,200 trips</text><rect x=\"681\" y=\"165\" width=\"284\" height=\"133\" rx=\"12\" fill=\"#fff1e8\" stroke=\"#d5e4ea\" stroke-width=\"1.3\"/><text x=\"823\" y=\"202\" font-size=\"17\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#b57b56\" font-weight=\"850\" font-family=\"system-ui,Segoe UI,Arial,sans-serif\">FESTIVAL WEEKEND</text><text x=\"823\" y=\"242\" font-size=\"17\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#57798a\" font-weight=\"720\" font-family=\"system-ui,Segoe UI,Arial,sans-serif\">Not an equal baseline</text><text x=\"52\" y=\"335\" font-size=\"17\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"#52798b\" font-weight=\"740\" font-family=\"system-ui,Segoe UI,Arial,sans-serif\">A stable reference can be versioned and seasonal; avoid accidentally mixing test periods.</text></svg></div><p class=\"ddm-note\">Training data can be a reference, but it is not automatically the best one.</p></section>"
    },
    {
      "stem": "How do statistical methods such as the Kolmogorov–Smirnov test, chi-square test and Population Stability Index (PSI) help detect changes in numerical and categorical features?",
      "tag": "Beginner · Drift measures",
      "answer": "d",
      "options": [
        "KS measures numerical drift using the difference between sample averages, chi-square compares category shares without considering counts, and PSI reports only the most changed fixed bin. Each detects a different shift.",
        "KS tests numerical distribution shape, chi-square compares category counts, and PSI compares fixed-bin proportions. Once any test crosses its threshold, the model's prediction accuracy is known to have declined.",
        "KS compares cumulative distributions and chi-square compares category counts, while PSI uses fixed bins. All three produce comparable p-values and can use the same 0.05 cutoff across sample sizes.",
        "KS compares numerical CDFs, chi-square tests category counts, and PSI compares fixed-bin proportions. Use suitable sample sizes and thresholds; none of these statistics directly measures prediction accuracy."
      ],
      "explanation": "<strong>Explanation:</strong> The two-sample Kolmogorov–Smirnov (KS) statistic finds the largest gap between numerical cumulative distributions. A chi-square homogeneity test can compare category frequencies, given suitable counts. PSI compares reference and current proportions across fixed bins, with careful handling of empty bins. For manufacturing sensors, KS can flag a temperature-distribution shift while a categorical test checks a change in device types. These methods describe data differences, not actual forecasting error. A small p-value is also not the same as a large business impact.",
      "figure": "<section class=\"ddm-figure\"><span class=\"ddm-kicker\">QUESTION 03 · VISUAL LESSON</span><div class=\"ddm-art\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 1000 430\" role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"ddm-head-3 ddm-desc-3\"><title id=\"ddm-head-3\">Different features call for different drift statistics</title><desc id=\"ddm-desc-3\">Overlaid cumulative distributions for numerical drift plus comparison of KS, chi-square, and population stability index; statistical significance differs from effect size.</desc><rect width=\"1000\" height=\"430\" fill=\"#f8fcfd\"/><text x=\"37\" y=\"40\" font-size=\"14\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"#268475\" font-weight=\"850\" font-family=\"system-ui,Segoe UI,Arial,sans-serif\">03 · DATA DRIFT MONITORING</text><text x=\"37\" y=\"79\" font-size=\"25\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"#2a5267\" font-weight=\"850\" font-family=\"system-ui,Segoe UI,Arial,sans-serif\">Different features call for different drift statistics</text><rect x=\"29\" y=\"111\" width=\"936\" height=\"269\" rx=\"12\" fill=\"#fff\" stroke=\"#d5e4ea\" stroke-width=\"1.3\"/><text x=\"54\" y=\"151\" font-size=\"15\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"#518095\" font-weight=\"850\" font-family=\"system-ui,Segoe UI,Arial,sans-serif\">NUMERICAL FEATURE · EMPIRICAL CUMULATIVE DISTRIBUTION</text><line x1=\"83\" y1=\"329\" x2=\"472\" y2=\"329\" stroke=\"#8eabb8\" stroke-width=\"2\" /><line x1=\"83\" y1=\"329\" x2=\"83\" y2=\"172\" stroke=\"#8eabb8\" stroke-width=\"2\" /><path d=\"M84 329 H130 V303 H180 V272 H235 V242 H290 V210 H355 V192 H454 V171\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#5dadd0\" stroke-width=\"4\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\"/><path d=\"M84 329 H208 V312 H266 V298 H315 V262 H376 V220 H426 V190 H454 V171\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#e8a270\" stroke-width=\"4\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\"/><text x=\"93\" y=\"355\" font-size=\"15\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"#5d7f8f\" font-weight=\"680\" font-family=\"system-ui,Segoe UI,Arial,sans-serif\">Feature value →</text><circle cx=\"114\" cy=\"390\" r=\"6\" fill=\"#5dadd0\"/><text x=\"127\" y=\"395\" font-size=\"15\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"#487c93\" font-weight=\"730\" font-family=\"system-ui,Segoe UI,Arial,sans-serif\">Reference</text><circle cx=\"259\" cy=\"390\" r=\"6\" fill=\"#e8a270\"/><text x=\"271\" y=\"395\" font-size=\"15\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"#976c52\" font-weight=\"730\" font-family=\"system-ui,Segoe UI,Arial,sans-serif\">Recent</text><line x1=\"290\" y1=\"210\" x2=\"290\" y2=\"262\" stroke=\"#bd8b67\" stroke-width=\"3\" /><text x=\"307\" y=\"252\" font-size=\"16\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"#a87856\" font-weight=\"740\" font-family=\"system-ui,Segoe UI,Arial,sans-serif\">Largest CDF gap</text><rect x=\"516\" y=\"153\" width=\"429\" height=\"207\" rx=\"12\" fill=\"#f2f9fb\" stroke=\"#d5e4ea\" stroke-width=\"1.3\"/><text x=\"540\" y=\"191\" font-size=\"16\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"#4a7f95\" font-weight=\"840\" font-family=\"system-ui,Segoe UI,Arial,sans-serif\">USE A METRIC SUITED TO EACH FEATURE</text><text x=\"544\" y=\"232\" font-size=\"19\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"#3e7589\" font-weight=\"770\" font-family=\"system-ui,Segoe UI,Arial,sans-serif\">KS  →  numeric distribution</text><text x=\"544\" y=\"270\" font-size=\"19\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"#3e7589\" font-weight=\"770\" font-family=\"system-ui,Segoe UI,Arial,sans-serif\">Chi-square  →  category counts</text><text x=\"544\" y=\"308\" font-size=\"19\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"#3e7589\" font-weight=\"770\" font-family=\"system-ui,Segoe UI,Arial,sans-serif\">PSI  →  binned proportions</text><text x=\"545\" y=\"342\" font-size=\"15\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"#5d808f\" font-weight=\"650\" font-family=\"system-ui,Segoe UI,Arial,sans-serif\">A p-value is not the size of the shift.</text></svg></div><p class=\"ddm-note\">KS uses cumulative distributions; category counts and fixed-bin proportions need different methods.</p></section>"
    },
    {
      "stem": "Why can Data Drift Monitoring miss important changes when it only checks global feature averages, and how do segment-level comparisons improve detection?",
      "tag": "Medium · Hidden segment drift",
      "answer": "b",
      "options": [
        "Global averages summarize all records, so an unchanged average proves that each customer segment has unchanged feature distributions. Segment-level comparisons add little unless an overall model metric already drops.",
        "Opposing changes in different groups can cancel in an overall average even as subgroup distributions move. Monitor meaningful segments and distribution shapes, while reporting sample sizes to avoid overreacting to noise.",
        "Segment-level drift checks are useful only when the groups have different classification labels. When labels are unavailable, stable overall averages guarantee stable values for every geographical or demographic segment.",
        "Global means can hide segment shifts, but comparing the means of each segment is sufficient to certify that their full distributions are stable. Sample size and population composition do not affect this conclusion."
      ],
      "explanation": "<strong>Explanation:</strong> An average can stay constant while important subgroups move in opposite directions. In the illustrated lending example, Region A's mean applicant age increases from 25 to 35 while Region B's falls from 65 to 55. With equally sized groups, the overall mean stays 45, masking both changes. Checking distributions within meaningful groups reveals the pattern. Report group sizes and examine more than averages because distributions can change shape without changing their means.",
      "figure": "<section class=\"ddm-figure\"><span class=\"ddm-kicker\">QUESTION 04 · VISUAL LESSON</span><div class=\"ddm-art\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 1000 430\" role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"ddm-head-4 ddm-desc-4\"><title id=\"ddm-head-4\">Segment drift can hide behind a stable overall average</title><desc id=\"ddm-desc-4\">Region A mean rises 25 to 35 and region B mean falls 65 to 55, while the equally weighted overall mean stays at 45.</desc><rect width=\"1000\" height=\"430\" fill=\"#f8fcfd\"/><text x=\"37\" y=\"40\" font-size=\"14\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"#268475\" font-weight=\"850\" font-family=\"system-ui,Segoe UI,Arial,sans-serif\">04 · DATA DRIFT MONITORING</text><text x=\"37\" y=\"79\" font-size=\"25\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"#2a5267\" font-weight=\"850\" font-family=\"system-ui,Segoe UI,Arial,sans-serif\">Segment drift can hide behind a stable overall average</text><rect x=\"29\" y=\"111\" width=\"936\" height=\"266\" rx=\"12\" fill=\"#fff\" stroke=\"#d5e4ea\" stroke-width=\"1.3\"/><text x=\"51\" y=\"150\" font-size=\"16\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"#548094\" font-weight=\"850\" font-family=\"system-ui,Segoe UI,Arial,sans-serif\">THE OVERALL MEAN CAN HIDE OPPOSITE GROUP MOVEMENTS</text><rect x=\"56\" y=\"170\" width=\"299\" height=\"159\" rx=\"12\" fill=\"#eaf7fc\" stroke=\"#d5e4ea\" stroke-width=\"1.3\"/><text x=\"78\" y=\"205\" font-size=\"17\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"#3387a0\" font-weight=\"850\" font-family=\"system-ui,Segoe UI,Arial,sans-serif\">REGION A</text><text x=\"80\" y=\"246\" font-size=\"27\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"#3387a0\" font-weight=\"850\" font-family=\"system-ui,Segoe UI,Arial,sans-serif\">25 → 35</text><text x=\"79\" y=\"285\" font-size=\"17\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"#617e8c\" font-weight=\"720\" font-family=\"system-ui,Segoe UI,Arial,sans-serif\">Change: +10</text><rect x=\"386\" y=\"170\" width=\"299\" height=\"159\" rx=\"12\" fill=\"#fff4e9\" stroke=\"#d5e4ea\" stroke-width=\"1.3\"/><text x=\"408\" y=\"205\" font-size=\"17\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"#c18b5e\" font-weight=\"850\" font-family=\"system-ui,Segoe UI,Arial,sans-serif\">REGION B</text><text x=\"410\" y=\"246\" font-size=\"27\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"#c18b5e\" font-weight=\"850\" font-family=\"system-ui,Segoe UI,Arial,sans-serif\">65 → 55</text><text x=\"409\" y=\"285\" font-size=\"17\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"#617e8c\" font-weight=\"720\" font-family=\"system-ui,Segoe UI,Arial,sans-serif\">Change: -10</text><rect x=\"715\" y=\"170\" width=\"229\" height=\"160\" rx=\"12\" fill=\"#eaf8f0\" stroke=\"#d5e4ea\" stroke-width=\"1.3\"/><text x=\"734\" y=\"207\" font-size=\"17\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"#318778\" font-weight=\"850\" font-family=\"system-ui,Segoe UI,Arial,sans-serif\">OVERALL MEAN</text><text x=\"734\" y=\"256\" font-size=\"26\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"#278679\" font-weight=\"860\" font-family=\"system-ui,Segoe UI,Arial,sans-serif\">45 → 45</text><text x=\"734\" y=\"296\" font-size=\"16\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"#557d88\" font-weight=\"700\" font-family=\"system-ui,Segoe UI,Arial,sans-serif\">No mean shift</text><text x=\"56\" y=\"356\" font-size=\"15\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"#617b8a\" font-weight=\"660\" font-family=\"system-ui,Segoe UI,Arial,sans-serif\">Equal group weights in this illustration; other distribution properties may still change.</text></svg></div><p class=\"ddm-note\">An unchanged overall mean does not imply an unchanged full distribution.</p></section>"
    },
    {
      "stem": "How can a short Python Data Drift Monitoring check compare numerical feature distributions and categorical proportions, and why must a detected shift be treated as an alert rather than proven performance loss?",
      "tag": "Medium · Python drift checks",
      "answer": "d",
      "options": [
        "Apply a KS test to the labels the model predicts, calculate category shares from its confidence scores, and use a p-value below the threshold as evidence that the deployed classifier's accuracy has declined.",
        "Compute a standardized difference between the two window means, then use that number as the measured prediction error rate. A categorical share change can be ignored because numerical features determine model accuracy.",
        "Compare only the last numerical observation with the reference maximum and report drift if it is outside that range. This measures distribution movement across whole windows without needing sample sizes or categoricals.",
        "Run a two-sample KS test on numerical reference and live values, then compare categorical shares. Flag drift for review; only ground-truth outcomes can confirm a predictive-quality decline."
      ],
      "explanation": "<strong>Explanation:</strong> The runnable Python example applies SciPy's two-sample KS test to numerical reference and current windows, then compares the share of app traffic across two categorical windows. The toy result is KS = 0.70 with p ≈ 0.0123, while app share rises from 30% to 70%. This flags a change, not a measured decline in accuracy. In ride-sharing data, such shifts could reflect a promotion or a broken collection pipeline. Investigate both possibilities before updating the model.",
      "figure": "<section class=\"ddm-figure\"><span class=\"ddm-kicker\">QUESTION 05 · VISUAL LESSON</span><div class=\"ddm-art\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 1000 430\" role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"ddm-head-5 ddm-desc-5\"><title id=\"ddm-head-5\">Python: numerical KS test plus categorical share check</title><desc id=\"ddm-desc-5\">Synthetic windows have KS statistic 0.70, p 0.0123, and app traffic proportion 30 to 70 percent; the alert signals investigation.</desc><rect width=\"1000\" height=\"430\" fill=\"#f8fcfd\"/><text x=\"37\" y=\"40\" font-size=\"14\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"#268475\" font-weight=\"850\" font-family=\"system-ui,Segoe UI,Arial,sans-serif\">05 · DATA DRIFT MONITORING</text><text x=\"37\" y=\"79\" font-size=\"25\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"#2a5267\" font-weight=\"850\" font-family=\"system-ui,Segoe UI,Arial,sans-serif\">Python: numerical KS test plus categorical share check</text><rect x=\"27\" y=\"111\" width=\"945\" height=\"272\" rx=\"12\" fill=\"#fff\" stroke=\"#d5e4ea\" stroke-width=\"1.3\"/><text x=\"53\" y=\"150\" font-size=\"17\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"#538196\" font-weight=\"850\" font-family=\"system-ui,Segoe UI,Arial,sans-serif\">TEST THE TWO WINDOWS · REVIEW THE RESULT</text><rect x=\"50\" y=\"176\" width=\"270\" height=\"127\" rx=\"12\" fill=\"#eaf7fc\" stroke=\"#d5e4ea\" stroke-width=\"1.3\"/><text x=\"71\" y=\"210\" font-size=\"17\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"#3e8199\" font-weight=\"850\" font-family=\"system-ui,Segoe UI,Arial,sans-serif\">NUMERIC FEATURE</text><text x=\"71\" y=\"244\" font-size=\"26\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"#367d94\" font-weight=\"860\" font-family=\"system-ui,Segoe UI,Arial,sans-serif\">KS = 0.70</text><text x=\"71\" y=\"279\" font-size=\"18\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"#557b8b\" font-weight=\"750\" font-family=\"system-ui,Segoe UI,Arial,sans-serif\">p-value ≈ 0.0123</text><line x1=\"326\" y1=\"236\" x2=\"355\" y2=\"236\" stroke=\"#638e9f\" stroke-width=\"3\" /><path d=\"M345 228 L355 236 L345 244\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#638e9f\" stroke-width=\"3\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\"/><rect x=\"367\" y=\"176\" width=\"247\" height=\"127\" rx=\"12\" fill=\"#ecf8f1\" stroke=\"#d5e4ea\" stroke-width=\"1.3\"/><text x=\"386\" y=\"210\" font-size=\"17\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"#2b8b78\" font-weight=\"850\" font-family=\"system-ui,Segoe UI,Arial,sans-serif\">CATEGORICAL</text><text x=\"386\" y=\"247\" font-size=\"21\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"#2a8578\" font-weight=\"860\" font-family=\"system-ui,Segoe UI,Arial,sans-serif\">App: 30% → 70%</text><text x=\"386\" y=\"281\" font-size=\"17\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"#567b86\" font-weight=\"710\" font-family=\"system-ui,Segoe UI,Arial,sans-serif\">Change: +40 pp</text><line x1=\"620\" y1=\"236\" x2=\"648\" y2=\"236\" stroke=\"#638e9f\" stroke-width=\"3\" /><path d=\"M638 228 L648 236 L638 244\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#638e9f\" stroke-width=\"3\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\"/><rect x=\"660\" y=\"176\" width=\"288\" height=\"127\" rx=\"12\" fill=\"#fff4e9\" stroke=\"#d5e4ea\" stroke-width=\"1.3\"/><text x=\"683\" y=\"210\" font-size=\"17\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"#ac7d57\" font-weight=\"850\" font-family=\"system-ui,Segoe UI,Arial,sans-serif\">DECISION</text><text x=\"683\" y=\"247\" font-size=\"20\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"#a77b55\" font-weight=\"820\" font-family=\"system-ui,Segoe UI,Arial,sans-serif\">Review feature drift</text><text x=\"683\" y=\"281\" font-size=\"17\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"#68818e\" font-weight=\"690\" font-family=\"system-ui,Segoe UI,Arial,sans-serif\">Not measured error</text><text x=\"57\" y=\"347\" font-size=\"16\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"#697f8a\" font-weight=\"680\" font-family=\"system-ui,Segoe UI,Arial,sans-serif\">Illustrative samples are tiny; the p-value threshold is NOT a production default.</text></svg></div><div class=\"ddm-code\"><div class=\"ddm-code-head\">Python · SciPy · 13 runnable lines</div><pre style=\"background:#1e1e1e;color:#f1f9ff;overflow-x:auto;white-space:pre\"><code style=\"display:block;background:transparent!important;color:#f1f9ff!important;border:0!important;padding:0!important;font:inherit;white-space:pre\">from collections import Counter\nfrom scipy.stats import ks_2samp\nreference = [40,42,43,45,46,49,50,52,54,55]\ncurrent = [46,50,51,57,58,60,61,63,64,66]\nold_channel = ['web']*7 + ['app']*3\nnew_channel = ['web']*3 + ['app']*7\nresult = ks_2samp(reference, current)\ndef share(rows, name):\n    return Counter(rows)[name] / len(rows)\ndelta = share(new_channel, 'app') - share(old_channel, 'app')\nprint(f'KS statistic: {result.statistic:.2f}, p-value: {result.pvalue:.4f}')\nprint(f'App share: {share(old_channel,&quot;app&quot;):.0%} -&gt; {share(new_channel,&quot;app&quot;):.0%}')\nprint(f'Change: {delta:+.0%}; flag for review: {result.pvalue &lt; .05}')</code></pre></div><p class=\"ddm-note\">The script uses tiny illustrative samples and a demonstration p-value threshold, not a production default.</p></section>"
    },
    {
      "stem": "How should drift thresholds and alert policies account for repeated feature tests, sample sizes, seasonality and business impact while limiting false alarms in production?",
      "tag": "Advanced · Calibrated alerting",
      "answer": "c",
      "options": [
        "Apply one fixed p-value threshold across monitored fields and page for every significant result. Repeated tests do not increase false-alarm risk, including during normal seasonal shifts.",
        "Raise alert sensitivity whenever the number of monitored features grows, because more features automatically make each significance test more reliable. Ignore sample size once the reference window has been fixed.",
        "Select representative baseline and recent windows, account for seasonality and sample size, and control repeated feature testing. Prioritize shift magnitude and business impact before escalating alerts.",
        "Use the largest drift result in the latest window as the threshold for all remaining features. This eliminates multiple-testing issues and ensures that alerts represent model degradation rather than mere input changes."
      ],
      "explanation": "<strong>Explanation:</strong> Frequent drift tests across many features can generate false alarms, especially with large datasets, repeated checks, and seasonal traffic. Calibrate thresholds against known stable periods and combine significance with effect size, sample size, affected segments and business impact. When testing many features, consider multiple-comparison controls and sensible alert aggregation. A music-streaming service might see weekend listening habits shift routinely. Rather than paging engineers for every change, it should distinguish expected weekend cycles from unusual device or data-pipeline behavior.",
      "figure": "<section class=\"ddm-figure\"><span class=\"ddm-kicker\">QUESTION 06 · VISUAL LESSON</span><div class=\"ddm-art\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 1000 430\" role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"ddm-head-6 ddm-desc-6\"><title id=\"ddm-head-6\">Many statistical checks create false-alarm risk</title><desc id=\"ddm-desc-6\">Ten monitored features with two small p-values; filtering by sampling, seasonality, multiple testing and impact avoids blind paging.</desc><rect width=\"1000\" height=\"430\" fill=\"#f8fcfd\"/><text x=\"37\" y=\"40\" font-size=\"14\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"#268475\" font-weight=\"850\" font-family=\"system-ui,Segoe UI,Arial,sans-serif\">06 · DATA DRIFT MONITORING</text><text x=\"37\" y=\"79\" font-size=\"25\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"#2a5267\" font-weight=\"850\" font-family=\"system-ui,Segoe UI,Arial,sans-serif\">Many statistical checks create false-alarm risk</text><rect x=\"29\" y=\"111\" width=\"938\" height=\"267\" rx=\"12\" fill=\"#fff\" stroke=\"#d5e4ea\" stroke-width=\"1.3\"/><text x=\"57\" y=\"151\" font-size=\"17\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"#508094\" font-weight=\"850\" font-family=\"system-ui,Segoe UI,Arial,sans-serif\">TEN FEATURE CHECKS · TWO APPARENT ALERTS</text><rect x=\"54\" y=\"166\" width=\"389\" height=\"31\" rx=\"7\" fill=\"#fff0e8\" stroke=\"#efd7c9\" stroke-width=\"1.3\"/><text x=\"72\" y=\"187\" font-size=\"16\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"#486d80\" font-weight=\"760\" font-family=\"system-ui,Segoe UI,Arial,sans-serif\">Age</text><text x=\"319\" y=\"187\" font-size=\"16\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"#b47a55\" font-weight=\"800\" font-family=\"system-ui,Segoe UI,Arial,sans-serif\">p = 0.012</text><rect x=\"54\" y=\"202\" width=\"389\" height=\"31\" rx=\"7\" fill=\"#fff0e8\" stroke=\"#efd7c9\" stroke-width=\"1.3\"/><text x=\"72\" y=\"223\" font-size=\"16\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"#486d80\" font-weight=\"760\" font-family=\"system-ui,Segoe UI,Arial,sans-serif\">Device</text><text x=\"319\" y=\"223\" font-size=\"16\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"#b47a55\" font-weight=\"800\" font-family=\"system-ui,Segoe UI,Arial,sans-serif\">p = 0.031</text><rect x=\"54\" y=\"238\" width=\"389\" height=\"31\" rx=\"7\" fill=\"#edf5f7\" stroke=\"#e0eaef\" stroke-width=\"1.3\"/><text x=\"72\" y=\"259\" font-size=\"16\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"#486d80\" font-weight=\"760\" font-family=\"system-ui,Segoe UI,Arial,sans-serif\">Locale</text><text x=\"319\" y=\"259\" font-size=\"16\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"#5a7f90\" font-weight=\"800\" font-family=\"system-ui,Segoe UI,Arial,sans-serif\">p = 0.21</text><rect x=\"54\" y=\"274\" width=\"389\" height=\"31\" rx=\"7\" fill=\"#edf5f7\" stroke=\"#e0eaef\" stroke-width=\"1.3\"/><text x=\"72\" y=\"295\" font-size=\"16\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"#486d80\" font-weight=\"760\" font-family=\"system-ui,Segoe UI,Arial,sans-serif\">Tenure</text><text x=\"319\" y=\"295\" font-size=\"16\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"#5a7f90\" font-weight=\"800\" font-family=\"system-ui,Segoe UI,Arial,sans-serif\">p = 0.64</text><text x=\"59\" y=\"361\" font-size=\"14\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"#708995\" font-weight=\"620\" font-family=\"system-ui,Segoe UI,Arial,sans-serif\">Six additional features not shown</text><line x1=\"464\" y1=\"244\" x2=\"499\" y2=\"244\" stroke=\"#638e9f\" stroke-width=\"3\" /><path d=\"M489 236 L499 244 L489 252\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#638e9f\" stroke-width=\"3\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\"/><rect x=\"509\" y=\"165\" width=\"434\" height=\"188\" rx=\"13\" fill=\"#ebf9f1\" stroke=\"#cce5da\" stroke-width=\"1.3\"/><text x=\"532\" y=\"202\" font-size=\"17\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"#338a77\" font-weight=\"850\" font-family=\"system-ui,Segoe UI,Arial,sans-serif\">BEFORE AN ALERT</text><text x=\"532\" y=\"238\" font-size=\"17\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"#4e7482\" font-weight=\"730\" font-family=\"system-ui,Segoe UI,Arial,sans-serif\">Enough samples?</text><text x=\"532\" y=\"268\" font-size=\"17\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"#4e7482\" font-weight=\"730\" font-family=\"system-ui,Segoe UI,Arial,sans-serif\">Seasonal shift expected?</text><text x=\"532\" y=\"298\" font-size=\"17\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"#4e7482\" font-weight=\"730\" font-family=\"system-ui,Segoe UI,Arial,sans-serif\">Multiple tests corrected?</text><text x=\"532\" y=\"327\" font-size=\"17\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"#4e7482\" font-weight=\"730\" font-family=\"system-ui,Segoe UI,Arial,sans-serif\">Meaningful business impact?</text></svg></div><p class=\"ddm-note\">A p-value, a shift magnitude and operational impact are different quantities.</p></section>"
    },
    {
      "stem": "How should an MLOps team respond when Data Drift Monitoring flags a production shift, including root-cause analysis, delayed labels, safe model changes and rollback controls?",
      "tag": "Advanced · Production response",
      "answer": "a",
      "options": [
        "Investigate seasonal, pipeline, and population changes, including affected groups and delayed outcome labels. Validate any fixes independently, then deploy gradually with rollback controls ready.",
        "Retrain with recent production features and available pseudo-labels whenever the shift exceeds a limit. New patterns mean the old decision boundary must be replaced before investigating measured performance.",
        "Refresh the baseline after verifying the shift persists, then close the alert without checking upstream feature transformations. This prevents repeated notifications while accepting the latest production population.",
        "Use the affected production slice to choose new model settings and judge the updated result, then deploy when its score improves. A separate test holdout can follow release."
      ],
      "explanation": "<strong>Explanation:</strong> A drift alert starts an investigation, not an automatic retraining order. Check whether the shift reflects seasonality, a schema error, a new user population or changes in data collection. Compare segments and evaluate prediction outcomes when labels arrive. If changes are needed, test them on independent data, use shadow or canary deployments, and retain rollback options. For traffic-camera recognition, a new rainy-season image pattern may require better data collection rather than immediate model replacement. Log evidence and assign an incident owner.",
      "figure": "<section class=\"ddm-figure\"><span class=\"ddm-kicker\">QUESTION 07 · VISUAL LESSON</span><div class=\"ddm-art\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 1000 430\" role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"ddm-head-7 ddm-desc-7\"><title id=\"ddm-head-7\">From drift alert to safe production action</title><desc id=\"ddm-desc-7\">An MLOps response chain detects distribution drift, diagnoses causes and impacted segments, validates a remedy, and deploys with rollback.</desc><rect width=\"1000\" height=\"430\" fill=\"#f8fcfd\"/><text x=\"37\" y=\"40\" font-size=\"14\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"#268475\" font-weight=\"850\" font-family=\"system-ui,Segoe UI,Arial,sans-serif\">07 · DATA DRIFT MONITORING</text><text x=\"37\" y=\"79\" font-size=\"25\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"#2a5267\" font-weight=\"850\" font-family=\"system-ui,Segoe UI,Arial,sans-serif\">From drift alert to safe production action</text><rect x=\"29\" y=\"111\" width=\"936\" height=\"267\" rx=\"12\" fill=\"#fff\" stroke=\"#d5e4ea\" stroke-width=\"1.3\"/><text x=\"53\" y=\"151\" font-size=\"16\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"#4c8196\" font-weight=\"850\" font-family=\"system-ui,Segoe UI,Arial,sans-serif\">ALERT IS THE START OF AN INVESTIGATION, NOT A RETRAIN COMMAND</text><rect x=\"50\" y=\"182\" width=\"202\" height=\"114\" rx=\"12\" fill=\"#ebf8fc\" stroke=\"#d5e4ea\" stroke-width=\"1.3\"/><text x=\"151\" y=\"219\" font-size=\"18\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#3b8199\" font-weight=\"850\" font-family=\"system-ui,Segoe UI,Arial,sans-serif\">DETECT</text><text x=\"151\" y=\"264\" font-size=\"16\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#567785\" font-weight=\"690\" font-family=\"system-ui,Segoe UI,Arial,sans-serif\">Shift + quality</text><rect x=\"284\" y=\"182\" width=\"202\" height=\"114\" rx=\"12\" fill=\"#f0f4ff\" stroke=\"#d5e4ea\" stroke-width=\"1.3\"/><text x=\"385\" y=\"219\" font-size=\"18\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#6b78a1\" font-weight=\"850\" font-family=\"system-ui,Segoe UI,Arial,sans-serif\">DIAGNOSE</text><text x=\"385\" y=\"264\" font-size=\"16\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#567785\" font-weight=\"690\" font-family=\"system-ui,Segoe UI,Arial,sans-serif\">Pipeline + slices</text><rect x=\"518\" y=\"182\" width=\"202\" height=\"114\" rx=\"12\" fill=\"#eaf8ef\" stroke=\"#d5e4ea\" stroke-width=\"1.3\"/><text x=\"619\" y=\"219\" font-size=\"18\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#318b75\" font-weight=\"850\" font-family=\"system-ui,Segoe UI,Arial,sans-serif\">VALIDATE</text><text x=\"619\" y=\"264\" font-size=\"16\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#567785\" font-weight=\"690\" font-family=\"system-ui,Segoe UI,Arial,sans-serif\">Labels + shadow</text><rect x=\"752\" y=\"182\" width=\"202\" height=\"114\" rx=\"12\" fill=\"#fff2e9\" stroke=\"#d5e4ea\" stroke-width=\"1.3\"/><text x=\"853\" y=\"219\" font-size=\"18\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#b58157\" font-weight=\"850\" font-family=\"system-ui,Segoe UI,Arial,sans-serif\">RELEASE</text><text x=\"853\" y=\"264\" font-size=\"16\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#567785\" font-weight=\"690\" font-family=\"system-ui,Segoe UI,Arial,sans-serif\">Canary + rollback</text><line x1=\"254\" y1=\"238\" x2=\"276\" y2=\"238\" stroke=\"#638e9f\" stroke-width=\"3\" /><path d=\"M266 230 L276 238 L266 246\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#638e9f\" stroke-width=\"3\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\"/><line x1=\"488\" y1=\"238\" x2=\"510\" y2=\"238\" stroke=\"#638e9f\" stroke-width=\"3\" /><path d=\"M500 230 L510 238 L500 246\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#638e9f\" stroke-width=\"3\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\"/><line x1=\"722\" y1=\"238\" x2=\"744\" y2=\"238\" stroke=\"#638e9f\" stroke-width=\"3\" /><path d=\"M734 230 L744 238 L734 246\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#638e9f\" stroke-width=\"3\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\"/><text x=\"57\" y=\"347\" font-size=\"17\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"#567b8b\" font-weight=\"720\" font-family=\"system-ui,Segoe UI,Arial,sans-serif\">Version the reference, log evidence, assign an owner, and confirm the fix actually helps.</text></svg></div><p class=\"ddm-note\">Detect → diagnose → verify actual impact → validate → controlled deployment.</p></section>"
    }
  ],
  "sources": "<details class=\"ddm-sources\"><summary>Further reading: drift monitoring methods</summary><ul><li><a href=\"https://learn.evidentlyai.com/ml-observability-course/module-4-designing-effective-ml-monitoring/how-to-choose-reference-dataset-ml-monitoring\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">Evidently: choosing a reference dataset</a></li><li><a href=\"https://learn.evidentlyai.com/ml-observability-course/module-2-ml-monitoring-metrics/data-prediction-drift-in-ml\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">Evidently: data and prediction drift</a></li><li><a href=\"https://nannyml.readthedocs.io/en/stable/tutorials/detecting_data_drift.html\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">NannyML: detecting data drift</a></li><li><a href=\"https://docs.scipy.org/doc/scipy/reference/generated/scipy.stats.ks_2samp.html\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">SciPy: two-sample KS statistic</a></li></ul></details>",
